research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r2-cex-exponential-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r.log-5-
research/audit/wave14-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6r2-cex-exponential-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r.log-6-BOUNDARY: The construction uses \(n\ge 1\), so every interval \([n,\,n+1/n]\) is nondegenerate and the mean value theorem applies. The exponential is differentiable on all of \(\mathbb{R}\), satisfying the MVT hypotheses. The limit \(\exp(n)/n\to\infty\) follows directly from the cited theorem with \(a=1,\ m=1\). No division by zero or empty-domain edge cases arise; the sequence begins at \(n=1\) and the proof needs only the positive tail where the limit behaviour is already established. All boundary conditions (zero, one, endpoints, nonempty choice, forward/reverse implications) were checked and found consistent.
